    <DataNode elementId="NIACINE" textLabel="nicotinate" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="PVNIIMVLHYAWGP-UHFFFAOYSA-M" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="508.25994873046875" centerY="1437.775390625" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, nilo-poyanco, SRI, caspi">|FRAME: NIACINE &quot;Nicotinate&quot;| (niacin, 3-pyridinecarboxylate) is a carboxylic derivative of |FRAME: PYRIDINE| that is found in nature as part of pyridine cofactors (e.g., NAD and NADP) and alkaloids (e.g., |FRAME: NICOTINE| and |FRAME: CPD-12317|). Nicotinate is one of three forms of vitamin B&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;: |FRAME: NIACINE|, |FRAME: NIACINAMIDE|, and |FRAME: NICOTINAMIDE_RIBOSE &quot;nicotinamide riboside&quot;| [more accurately named 1-(&amp;beta;-D ribofuranosyl)nicotinamide]. These three forms are interchangeable, and are usually used for the biosynthesis of |FRAME: NAD| |CITS: [Elvehjem38][15137942]|. |FRAME: NICOTINAMIDE_RIBOSE| can be converted to |FRAME: NIACINAMIDE| by |FRAME: EC-3.2.2.3| |FRAME: RXN-8441| and |FRAME: NIACINAMIDE| can be hydrolyzed to |FRAME: NIACINE| by |FRAME: EC-3.5.1.19| (which is not present in humans) |FRAME: NICOTINAMID-RXN| Eukaryotes and prokaryotes produce NAD &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; from |FRAME: TRP| and |FRAME: L-ASPARTATE|, respectively, by pathways that bypasses vitamin B3 compounds and instead proceed via |FRAME: NICOTINATE_NUCLEOTIDE| (see |FRAME: NADSYN-PWY| and |FRAME: PYRIDNUCSYN-PWY|). Vitamin B3 compounds are used in NAD salvage pathways such as |FRAME: PWY-5381| and |FRAME: PWY3O-4106| |CITS: [13563526][13563527][15137942]|. In populations with a tryptophan-poor diet the NAD &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; biosynthesis pathway is not sufficient to produce the required NAD, making the salvage pathways that rely on vitamin B&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; compounds the main source of NAD. Under these conditions a difficiency of vitamin B&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; compounds can result in the disease pellagra.</Comment>
